ok - mixed leased/unleased aliases refuse with upgrade and repair guidance, and no status read, in both entry orders on an unproven provider
ok - escaped or relative entry paths cannot prove a slot held beside a clean leased alias
ok - jq checks unleased aliases; without jq the check refuses before alias logic
ok - prefiltered leased aliases and duplicate unleased names require repair, not a lease
ok - the shared public allocation guard tells a jq-free caller to install jq
ok - symlink unleased alias beside a leased entry prevents a held verdict
ok - case-variant unleased alias beside a leased entry prevents a held verdict
ok - all recovered-lease path spellings beside a leased entry remain unsafe off a Firstmate home
ok - a pool slot holding a live record's nested current copy refuses allocation until that record is retired
ok - conflicting recovered-lease aliases refuse a spawn before allocation without changing pool state
ok - unsupported entry refusals identify the entry and path, and require removal or renaming, never a lease
ok - a pool slot whose hold Treehouse's state cannot prove still refuses allocation and names how to inspect or hold it
ok - a recovered lease keeps refusing allocation on a treehouse that does not prove persistent recovered leases, names the upgrade, and runs no pool command
ok - an ordinary lease with a lease id is admitted without the proof; id-less, recovery-reason, recovered-prefix, holderless and duplicate entries refuse, and with the proof only an entry on the published protected holder is admitted
ok - a failed fresh read refuses on an unproven provider (an ordinary entry still admits), and a whitespace-only version is not a proof
ok - an unproven provider is never asked for status to place an unleased slot (the refusal names the upgrade), while a proven provider may use the listing
ok - a recorded copy in a pool slot with no state file refuses (pool-dir evidence), while a copy under the project's own checkout and a home with no pool use still spawn
ok - a recorded copy in a linked worktree with no state file refuses when the pool is evidenced by a .treehouse path or by treehouse pool-dir, and when pool-dir cannot say; it is not taken for a pool slot only when pool-dir names another folder
ok - the preservation fingerprint reads the nested copy's HEAD: a same-bytes HEAD move changes it, and the old enclosing-slot-only fingerprint did not
ok - the lease-entry oracle accepts the named holder rewrite and unrelated slots changing, and rejects a change to the lease id, lease time, leased flag, recovery reason, owner fields or a second entry
ok - the real-provider fixture wrapper refuses a treehouse get with no explicit --root before the binary runs, and passes rooted gets and other commands through unchanged
ok - a capability answer whose holder is the old holder plus a newline, or only a newline, is not proof and cannot make an old-holder slot pass; a distinct protected holder still needs its adoption
ok - with a proven persistent-recovered-lease capability a recovered slot on the protected holder holding a current copy stays untouched while the spawn takes the free slot, whatever its root's state or state order
ok - a proven capability relaxes only the recovered lease: unleased, missing, and unreadable entries still refuse
ok - an old holder adopted under the protected holder survives an old session's exit before and after the allocation, with the new owner's lease and copy intact
ok - an old holder the provider did not adopt refuses before treehouse get with a named cause, and adoption runs once per process
ok - the shared guard bin/fm-home-seed.sh runs refuses an old recovered holder until the provider adopts it, and passes beside a protected slot
ok - the combined rollout keeps the recovered copy and the new owner's copy intact at every step
ok - the spawn's own treehouse get is executed against the private pool with an explicit root, the test reads the slot that command chose, the recovered slot is byte-preserved, and a get that hands out the recovered slot is detected
skip - real-provider recovered-slot allocation: FM_TEST_TREEHOUSE_PROVIDER names no executable treehouse; the case did NOT run
ok - a nested current copy inside a slot Treehouse's state leases does not stop an unrelated spawn
ok - a spawn never receives a superseded slot holding a nested copy, and refuses before treehouse get once Treehouse names that folder current
ok - an ordinary leased secondmate home slot holding another project's task copy does not stop a spawn, and a recovered one gets no exemption on an unproven provider
ok - a pool slot a reassociated task left is acquired and claimed by a new task
ok - an unavailable registered local home is named in one warning and the spawn proceeds
